{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction for criminal conviction or civil liability (NJ Eviction Law)","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of, or civilly liable for, assault, threats, theft, drug or human trafficking offenses on the premises, or who lets such a person live there. Suit must be filed within two years; a three-day Notice to Quit is required.","key_value":"3-day Notice to Quit; 2-year filing limit","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies in New Jersey, including leased premises, buildings, complexes and mobile home parks.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Statewide residential rental premises and mobile home parks"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may file for eviction, if the tenant has been convicted of or pleaded guilty to, or if a juvenile has been found to be delinquent by the Court due to an offense involving theft of property from the landlord or from tenants residing in the same building or complex.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction causes and notice for owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings may be removed only by court order, and only for the causes listed in the Eviction Law. Notice is three months for at-will or year-to-year tenancies, one month for month-to-month, and three days for disorderly, destructive or rule-violating conduct.","key_value":"3-month notice (at-will/year-to-year); 1-month (month-to-month); 3-day for disorderly/destructive/rule violations","coverage_conditions":"Landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings in New Jersey.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house evictions: good cause and welfare board notice","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to the County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Rooming and boarding houses: buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Rooming and boarding houses"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction by owner occupancy or removal from residential use","requirement":"A landlord who evicted a tenant for personal occupancy must occupy the unit at least six months; one who evicted to demolish or retire the building from residential use may not use it for housing for five years. Violators owe treble damages, fees and up to $10,000 per violation.","key_value":"6-month owner occupancy; 5-year residential-use bar; treble damages; civil penalty up to $10,000 per violation","coverage_conditions":"Landlords who obtained eviction by giving notice of personal occupancy or of permanent removal of the property from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Landlords who evicted for owner occupancy or removal from residential use"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Former tenant may recover three times the damages plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000 for each violation of the five-year bar; property may not be registered as a planned real estate development for five years.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the  landlord wishes to personally occupy the unit the landlord must occupy the unit for at least six (6) months.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-reprisal protection against eviction or non-renewal","requirement":"A landlord may not evict, substantially alter a lease or its terms, or refuse to renew when a tenant enforces lease or legal rights, complains to a government authority about health or safety violations, organizes or joins a tenant organization, or refuses a retaliatory lease change.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"All dwelling rentals including mobile homes"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"Tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure eviction: 90-day notice to quit","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction. Before evicting a tenant because a buyer of the foreclosed property wants to occupy it as a primary residence, the landlord must give a 90-day notice to quit, and may not act before an existing lease expires.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants in foreclosed properties; month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential tenants in foreclosed properties"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69 et seq.","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":"Federal foreclosure law does not preempt State or local law providing longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy","requirement":"Tenants at least 62, permanently disabled, or certain disabled veterans who lived in the building at least one year before the conversion recording may be protected from eviction for 40 years if family income is no more than three times the county per-person average or $50,000, whichever is greater.","key_value":"40 years' protection; income limit 3 times county per-person average or $50,000, whichever is greater","coverage_conditions":"Tenants in buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership who meet the age, disability or veteran criteria and the one-year residency and income limits.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Condominium, cooperative or fee simple conversions; tenants 62+, disabled, or certain disabled veterans with 1 year of residency"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 (qualified county conversions)","requirement":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 extends protected tenancy to qualified tenants in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible under the 1981 Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act; currently only Hudson County is a qualified county.","key_value":"Hudson County only","coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in buildings converted or being converted in a qualified county; at present only Hudson County.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Qualified counties; currently only Hudson County"},"exemptions":"Tenants who were eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons under the 1981 Act.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
